      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Political Struggles and Lebanese Civil War</strong><br>\nDuring the 1970s and early 1980s, Arafat faced multiple challenges, including a civil war in Lebanon, where the PLO was based. The organization was involved in fighting against Israel and internal Lebanese factions. In 1982, Israel invaded Lebanon to destroy PLO bases, which led to a siege of Beirut. Arafat and the PLO were eventually forced to evacuate the city.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The First Intifada</strong><br>\nThe First Intifada, a Palestinian uprising against Israeli occupation in the West Bank and Gaza Strip, began in 1987. Arafat supported the intifada by advocating for Palestinian self-determination. This period marked a shift in Arafat's tactics from armed conflict to diplomatic negotiations as a way to resolve the Palestinian-Israeli conflict.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Oslo Accords</strong><br>\nArafat's role in the historic Oslo Accords of 1993 was a turning point. He negotiated directly with Israel, which led to mutual recognition between the PLO and Israel. The accords were supposed to be a step towards peace and the creation of a Palestinian state. Arafat received the Nobel Peace Prize along with Israeli leaders for his efforts.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
