          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Establishing the Palestinian Authority</strong><br>\nAfter the Oslo Accords, the Palestinian Authority (PA) was established, and Arafat was elected its first president in 1996. The PA was meant to govern parts of the West Bank and Gaza Strip as an interim body until a final agreement was reached. Arafat's governance was marked by efforts to build institutions for a future state but also faced criticism for authoritarianism and corruption.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Camp David Summit</strong><br>\nIn 2000, Arafat attended the Camp David Summit, a meeting hosted by the United States to negotiate a \"final status\" settlement between Palestinians and Israelis. The summit ended without an agreement, and Arafat was blamed by many for the failure. He claimed the offers did not meet basic Palestinian rights, including the status of Jerusalem and the right of return for refugees.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nArafat died in 2004 under mysterious circumstances, with many Palestinians believing he was poisoned, though no conclusive evidence has been provided. His death marked the end of an era for Palestinian politics. Arafat remains a controversial figure: a symbol of resistance and hope for Palestinians, and a figure of terrorism and obstruction to peace for many Israelis. His legacy is integral to the ongoing dialogue about peace and the future of the Palestinian people.</p>\n"
              },
